WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
In a research setting, a conflict of
interest functions as an equation of

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.620
competing priorities. On one side are
primary duties—research objectivity,

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.720
public welfare, and client protection.

4
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.740
On the other side are secondary
interests—personal financial gain,

5
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.680
career advancement, relationships, and
reputation.

6
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.140
A conflict exists when the pursuit of
these secondary interests exerts

7
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.360
pressure that could undermine the primary
professional duties.

8
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.480
To manage these variables, researchers use
a five-step protocol.

9
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.640
It begins by defining the specific COI and
identifying risks across funding,

10
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.500
authorship, and data access. The process
continues by interpreting guidelines,

11
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.620
applying management plans, and evaluating
the impacts on integrity within REC

12
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.280
protocols. Open science practices are
integrated into this evaluation phase to

13
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.800
reduce potential bias. Utilizing this
framework transforms ethical oversight

14
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.940
from a reactive measure into a normalized,
proactive defense mechanism.

15
00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.696
We can track exactly how external and
internal pressures enter the system.
